{
  "ID": "9.5",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "We applied proteomics-based NMF subtyping strategies to the IPMNs and PDACs to explore tumor heterogeneities and reveal progression features. This analysis showed three clusters, NMF 1 was entirely composed of PDACs. NMF 2 included some PDACs and some IPMNs, while NMF 3 was almost entirely composed of IPMNs with only one PDAC. Most (75%) IPMNs with an associated PDAC were clustered in NMF 2. Use the feature genes in each of the NMF clusters and pairwise comparison between clusters to interpret the differences between NMF clusters and provide a summary of functional and clinical implications.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-15 20:28:13",
  "elapsed_seconds": 302.63,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Introduction/Context  \nWang et al. applied proteomics and glycoproteomics to 64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs, matched normal ducts, and cyst fluids, then used NMF to define three proteomic subtypes. NMF 1 is composed exclusively of PDACs, NMF 3 almost entirely of IPMNs (one PDAC), and NMF 2 a mixture (including 75% of IPMNs that progressed to PDAC). Four DE contrasts (NMF 3 vs 1, NMF 3 vs 2, NMF 2 vs 1, NMF 1 vs 2) and cluster-defining feature lists (Table S4G) are available. Tissue source: surgically resected pancreas; assay: macrodissected proteomics/glycoproteomics; exact sample‐size per contrast: not specified.\n\nKeywords  \nIPMN; PDAC; mitochondrial metabolism; glycosylation; proliferation\n\nCandidate Genes  \nNDUFA4; PMM2; PGRMC1; AKR1B15; LGALS8\n\nSummary of Cluster Features and Pairwise Comparisons  \n– NMF 1 (PDAC)—693 feature genes enriched in cytoskeletal remodeling and cell‐cycle regulators (e.g., KIF2A, PDLIM1, DNM1L). Pairwise, NMF 1 vs 2 shows stronger expression of SH3PXD2B and PGP, consistent with invasive motility and proteasome activity.  \n– NMF 2 (mixed)—627 features centered on vesicle trafficking, ER stress and glycoprotein processing (e.g., AP3B1, STXBP3, SUPT5H), bridging benign IPMN and malignant PDAC. NMF 2 vs 1 DE highlights apolipoprotein (IGKV1D-17, APOM) and lipid metabolism shifts.  \n– NMF 3 (IPMN)—456 features dominated by mitochondrial electron transport and glycan‐biosynthesis enzymes (e.g., NDUFA4, COX7A2L, PMM2, AKR1B15). In NMF 3 vs 1 and vs 2, OXPHOS subunits (NDUFS3/5/6/7/8), DPM1/GMDS (glycosylation) and PGRMC1 are highly up.  \n\nFunctional and Clinical Implications  \nNMF 3’s enrichment for mitochondrial and glycosylation pathways suggests an early metabolic reprogramming in non‐invasive IPMNs that may be targetable with metabolic or glycosylation‐inhibitors. NMF 1’s cell‐cycle/cytoskeletal signature reflects fully invasive PDAC biology, indicating reliance on proliferative and proteasomal processes. NMF 2 captures a transitional proteome, combining vesicle/ER stress-related glycoprotein machinery with incipient lipid-metabolic and immune‐modulatory changes, consistent with lesions on the brink of invasion (75% of IPMNs associated with PDAC). Clinically, cluster-specific biomarkers (e.g., PGRMC1/PMM2 in NMF 3 cyst fluid; SH3PXD2B/PGP in NMF 1 tissue) could guide risk stratification and tailored therapeutics.\n\nNext Steps & Clarifications  \n1. Validate PGRMC1 and NDUFA4 as cyst‐fluid biomarkers in an independent IPMN cohort.  \n2. Functionally test metabolic inhibitors (OXPHOS or PMM2 inhibitors) on NMF 3-like organoids.  \n3. Profile glycan structures (via LC–MS) on NMF 2 vs 3 cyst fluids to confirm glycoprotein processing signatures.  \n4. Explicitly assess stress‐response and replication/cell‐cycle gene modules in each NMF subtype (not directly specified here).\n\nReferences  \nNone.",
